        {
          "comment": "  /* ── Hardware markings ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n     The colors PRINTED ON THE PHYSICAL PADS, reproduced on the controller\n     art. Theme-independent on purpose: a real Xbox B is that red under any\n     stylesheet, so these do not belong in either theme block.\n\n     These are NOT the palette and NOT slot identity. Slot identity is\n     `--cool` and only `--cool` — coloring slot 2 red because P2 is red on\n     the mark would read as \"slot 2 has failed\", since red means danger\n     everywhere else in this app.\n\n     CONTRAST EXEMPTION, deliberate: `--hw-xbox-b` and `--hw-xbox-x` carry\n     white letters at 4.00:1 and 3.94:1, under the 4.5 floor. They stay,\n     because this is a PICTURE of a device — repainting it would make the\n     drawing wrong — and because the letter is not the accessible name:\n     every hit zone gets an `aria-label` naming the control and every key\n     bound to it (`render_map.rs`, `zone_label`). The floor applies to text\n     that carries meaning; this glyph is decoration on top of a label that\n     already exists. The contrast gate encodes this exemption explicitly so\n     it is a decision on record, not an oversight. */"
        },
        {
          "cssName": "--hw-xbox-a",
          "$value": "#57c04a",
          "$type": "color"
        },
        {
          "cssName": "--hw-xbox-a-on",
          "$value": "#08170a",
          "$type": "color"
        },
        {
          "cssName": "--hw-xbox-b",
          "$value": "#e04b3f",
          "$type": "color"
        },
        {
          "cssName": "--hw-xbox-x",
          "$value": "#3f7fe0",
          "$type": "color"
        },
        {
          "cssName": "--hw-xbox-y",
          "$value": "#f0b429",
          "$type": "color"
        },
        {
          "cssName": "--hw-xbox-y-on",
          "$value": "#201603",
          "$type": "color"
        },
        {
          "cssName": "--hw-xbox-on",
          "$value": "#ffffff",
          "$description": "B and X take white, as the hardware does",
          "$type": "color"
        },
        {
          "comment": "  /* The DS4 prints colored SHAPES on grey plastic, so the glyph carries the\n     color and the disc stays neutral. The disc is the one value here that is\n     ours rather than the hardware's — it was blue-grey (#2b3346), which read\n     as a second hue against a violet ground; it is now the same violet-grey\n     family as the rest of the page. All four glyphs gained contrast on it\n     (4.60→5.17 worst, the cross). */"
